\cf2 Kathy tapped Chanta on the shoulder. \'93Those Elvines you were talking to before\'97you know them?\'94\
Chanta was perplexed to the exact wattage that the light bulb of his brain would allow.\'93I know a lot of Elvines, Kathy.\'94\
Kathy pursed her lips as she watched the group Chanta had been talking with. \'93Do you know them, or 
\f2\i know
\f0\i0  them?\
\'93I\'92m the Great Teacher and possibly the richest cleaner in the history of the world.\'94\
\'93Your old friends like you better when you were neither.\'94\
Chant shrugged. \'93I still like you, Kathy, even if you like someone else more now.\'94 He tried not to glance at Hayley. \'93But I just meant, for anyone that is both of those things, one knowing leads to the other.\'94\
Kathy held her breath and counted the busted globes in the chandeliers.\
The house lights went down and three spots focussed on the spa bath where  a Clambake-series Elvis called Tom Wilson climbed two wooden steps that had been screwed to the side of the spa bath and stood on the mosaics, where the mermaids had been looking world-weary since before anybody in the room was born.\
\'93My fellow Elvi,\'94 Tom said. \'93Praise the Dawkins.\'94\
